REGIONAL REGULATION OF SINGKAWANG CITY

NUMBER 4 OF 2017

CONCERNING

PROTECTION OF CHILDREN AND WOMEN

BY THE GRACE OF ALMIGHTY GOD

MAYOR OF SINGKAWANG,

Considering:

a. that children and women as creations of Almighty God need to be protected and their honor, dignity, and self-esteem safeguarded from all forms of discrimination, acts of violence, and exploitation to improve the quality of life of children and women physically, spiritually, and socially in realizing an advanced, just, prosperous, welfare, and democratic society based on Pancasila and the 1945 Constitution of the Republic of Indonesia;
b. that the fate of children and women in Singkawang City needs attention from the Regional Government and society because cases of violence against children and women still occur, and children in conflict with the law, so efforts are needed to provide legal protection for children and women;
c. that to provide protection for children and women, also as implementation of higher Laws and Regulations and constitutes one of the mandatory government affairs that is the authority of the Regional Government, the regulation of protection of children and women needs to be regulated in a Regional Regulation;
d. that based on the considerations as referred to in letter a, letter b, and letter c, it is necessary to establish a Regional Regulation concerning Protection of Children and Women;

In View of:

1. Article 18 paragraph (6) of the 1945 Constitution of the Republic of Indonesia;
2. Law Number 4 of 1979 concerning Child Welfare (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 1979 Number 32, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 3143);
3. Law Number 7 of 1984 concerning Ratification of the Convention on the Elimination of All Forms of Discrimination Against Women (Convention On the Elimination of All Form of Discrimination Against Women) (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 1984 Number 29, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 3277);
4. Law Number 39 of 1999 concerning Human Rights (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 1999 Number 165, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 3886);
5. Law Number 12 of 2001 concerning Establishment of Singkawang City (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2001 Number 92, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4119);
6. Law Number 23 of 2002 concerning Child Protection (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2002 Number 109, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4235) as amended several times most recently by Law Number 17 of 2016 concerning Stipulation of Government Regulation in Lieu of Law Number 1 of 2016 concerning Second Amendment to Law Number 23 of 2002 concerning Child Protection into Law (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2016 Number 237, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5946);
7. Law Number 23 of 2004 concerning Elimination of Domestic Violence (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2004 Number 95, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4419);
8. Law Number 14 of 2009 concerning Ratification of Protocol To Prevent, Suppress and Punish Trafficking In Persons, Especially Women and Children, Supplementing the United Nation Convention Against Transnational Organized Crime (Protocol to Prevent, Suppress, and Punish Trafficking in Persons, Especially Women and Children, Supplementing the United Nations Convention Against Transnational Organized Crime) (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2009 Number 53, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4990);
9. Law Number 10 of 2012 concerning Ratification of Optional Protocol to the Convention on the Rights of the Child on the Sale of Children, Child Prostitution and Child Pornography (Optional Protocol to the Convention on the Rights of the Child on the Sale of Children, Child Prostitution and Child Pornography (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2012 Number 149, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5330);
10. Law Number 11 of 2012 concerning Juvenile Criminal Justice System (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2012 Number 153, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5332);
11. Law Number 23 of 2014 concerning Regional Government (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2014 Number 244, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5587) as amended several times most recently by Law Number 9 of 2015 concerning Second Amendment to Law Number 23 of 2014 concerning Regional Government (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2015 Number 58, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5679);
12. Government Regulation Number 4 of 2006 concerning Implementation and Cooperation for Recovery of Victims of Domestic Violence (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2006 Number 15, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 64);
13. Government Regulation Number 9 of 2008 concerning Procedures and Mechanisms for Integrated Services for Witnesses and/or Victims of Criminal Acts of Trafficking in Persons (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2008 Number 22, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4818);
14. Presidential Regulation Number 69 of 2008 concerning Task Force for Prevention and Handling of Criminal Acts of Trafficking in Persons;
15. Presidential Regulation Number 18 of 2014 concerning Protection and Empowerment of Women and Children in Social Conflict (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2014 Number 44);
16. Regulation of the State Minister for Women's Empowerment Number 3 of 2008 concerning Guidelines for Implementation of Child Protection;
17. Regulation of the State Minister for Women's Empowerment and Child Protection Number 5 of 2010 concerning Guidelines for Establishment and Development of Integrated Service Centers (State News of the Republic of Indonesia of 2010 Number 446);
18. Regulation of the State Minister for Women's Empowerment and Child Protection Number 1 of 2011 concerning National Socio-Cultural Strategy to Realize Gender Equality (State News of the Republic of Indonesia of 2012 Number 168);
19. Regulation of the State Minister for Women's Empowerment and Child Protection Number 11 of 2011 concerning Policy for Development of Child-Friendly Regencies/Cities (State News of the Republic of Indonesia of 2012 Number 168);
20. Regulation of the State Minister for Women's Empowerment and Child Protection Number 12 of 2011 concerning Indicators of Child-Friendly Regencies/Cities (State News of the Republic of Indonesia of 2012 Number 169);
21. Regulation of the State Minister for Women's Empowerment and Child Protection Number 10 of 2012 concerning Guidelines for Establishment and Strengthening of Task Force for Prevention and Handling of Criminal Acts of Trafficking in Persons (State News of the Republic of Indonesia of 2012 Number 984);
22. Regulation of the Minister for Women's Empowerment and Child Protection Number 6 of 2015 concerning System for Women's Empowerment and Child Protection (State News of the Republic of Indonesia of 2015 Number 615);
23. Regional Regulation Number 3 of 2015 concerning Implementation of Protection of Women from Acts of Violence (Regional Gazette of West Kalimantan Province of 2015 Number 3, Supplement to the Regional Gazette of West Kalimantan Province Number 3);
24. Regional Regulation Number 4 of 2015 concerning Child Protection (Regional Gazette of West Kalimantan Province of 2015 Number 4, Supplement to the Regional Gazette of West Kalimantan Province Number 4);

With Mutual Approval

REGIONAL HOUSE OF REPRESENTATIVES OF SINGKAWANG CITY

and

MAYOR OF SINGKAWANG

HAS DECIDED:

To Establish:

REGIONAL REGULATION CONCERNING PROTECTION OF CHILDREN AND WOMEN.

CHAPTER II

RIGHTS OF CHILDREN, WOMEN, AND VICTIMS

Article 2

( 1) Every child has the right:
a. to live, grow, develop, and participate reasonably in accordance with human dignity and worth, and to receive protection from criminal acts, violence, exploitation, and neglect;
b. to a name as personal identity and citizenship status;
c. to worship according to their religion under parental guidance and to practice the teachings of their religion;
d. to know their parents, be raised, and cared for by their own parents;
e. to obtain health services and social security in accordance with physical, mental, spiritual, and social needs;
f. to obtain education and teaching in accordance with age level, physical and mental condition, intelligence as well as interests and talents;
g. to express and have their opinion heard as well as to receive, seek, and provide information in accordance with the level of intelligence and age for self-development in accordance with moral values and propriety;
h. to rest and utilize leisure time, play, express themselves for self-development;
1. to receive proper care from parents/guardians or other parties responsible for their care;
J. to obtain protection from abuse in politics, involvement in armed conflicts, involvement in social unrest, involvement in events containing elements of violence, involvement in warfare, being targets of persecution, torture or imposition of inhumane punishment and involvement of children in the worst forms of work;
k. to obtain protection from the dangers of narcotics, pornography, and violent shows or other things that impact changes in child development;
I. to have contact with parents if separated; and
m. to obtain other rights in accordance with Laws and Regulations.
2. Every woman has the right:
a. to live, maintain life, and improve living standards;
b. to live peacefully, safely, in peace, happily, prosperously both physically and spiritually;
c. to enjoy a good and healthy living environment;
d. to live in a family within a legal marriage bond based on the provisions of Laws and Regulations;
e. to obtain work according to ability, conditions as well as fair and just wages;
f. specifically for women with disabilities, the right to receive facilities and special treatment in public services;
g. to play an active role in the political and governmental fields in accordance with their potential and capabilities;
h. to receive legal information and services; and
i. to obtain other rights in accordance with human dignity and based on the provisions of Laws and Regulations.
3. Every victim has the right to receive:
a. protection;
b. information;
c. optimal services;
d . continuous handling up to the rehabilitation stage;
e. confidential handling;
f. assistance in accordance with applicable provisions;
g. guarantee of rights related to the victim's status as a family member as well as a community member;
h . to obtain restitution in accordance with the provisions of Laws and Regulations; and
i. other rights regulated in the provisions of Laws and Regulations.

Article 3

Child victims of violence in addition to receiving rights as referred to in Article 2 paragraph (1), and paragraph (3) also receive special rights as follows:
a. the right to respect and full use for survival, growth, and development;
b. the right to basic services;
c. the right to equal protection;
d. the right to be free from various stigma; and
e. the right to obtain freedom.

CHAPTER III

OBLIGATIONS OF CHILDREN

Article 6

Every child is obligated to:
a. respect parents, guardians, and teachers;
b. love family, community, and care for friends;
c. love the homeland, nation, and state;
d. perform worship according to the teachings of their religion; and
e. practice noble ethics and morals.

JDIH Kota Singkawang

Article 8

1. The obligations and responsibilities of the Regional Government as referred to in Article 7 are implemented by:
a. formulating and implementing policies, programs, and activities for child and women protection;
b. ensuring the establishment and implementation of cooperation between various parties in the Region, with and between various parties in regencies/cities and all related elements in the implementation of child and women protection;
c. providing support for facilities, infrastructure, and availability of human resources in organizing child and women protection;
d. fulfilling, protecting, and respecting children's rights as well as ensuring children to use their rights in expressing opinions in accordance with the child's age and intelligence; and
e. fostering and supervising the organization of child and women protection.
2. The obligations and responsibilities of the Regional Government as referred to in paragraph (1) are coordinated by the Regional Apparatus Organization responsible for women's empowerment and child protection affairs.
3. In order to carry out the obligations and responsibilities as referred to in paragraph (1), the Regional Government establishes programs and action activities for child and women protection in 1 (one) RAD as the basis for Regional Apparatus Organizations in implementing child and women protection in the region.
4. The RAD as referred to in paragraph (3) is part of the Regional Medium-Term Development Plan regulated by Mayor Regulation.

Article 11

1. The obligations and responsibilities of parents as referred to in Article 7, include:
a. nurturing, caring for, educating, providing a sense of security and not committing mistreatment and violence against children;
b. developing children according to their abilities, talents, and interests;
c. reporting every birth of a child to the authorized agency to conduct birth registration;
d. preventing marriage at child age; and
e. providing character education and instilling moral values in children.
2. In the event that parents do not exist, or their whereabouts are unknown, or due to a certain reason cannot carry out their obligations and responsibilities, then the obligations and responsibilities as referred to in paragraph (1) may be transferred to the family, which is implemented in accordance with the provisions of Laws and Regulations.
3. In the event that the parents and family of the child cannot carry out their obligations and responsibilities, then the obligations and responsibilities as referred to in paragraph (1), and paragraph (2), are transferred to a person or legal entity that meets the requirements appointed as guardian of the child concerned.

Article 20

Implementation of protection of children and women is carried out by:
a. planning and development of information systems for children and women;
b. prevention and protection;
c. protection of children;
d. special protection;
e. protection of women;
f. protection in the workplace;
g. handling, and recovery of victims of violence;
h. rehabilitation, reintegration, and empowerment;
i. law enforcement services, and legal aid; and
j. facilitation of juvenile justice.

Article 22

1. Regional Government is obliged to undertake prevention and protection efforts against violent treatment, exploitation, discrimination, maltreatment, and neglect.
2. Implementation of prevention and protection as referred to in paragraph (1), may cooperate with educational institutions, religious institutions, mass media, professional organizations, business entities, and/or community institutions.
3. Prevention and protection efforts as referred to in paragraph (2) are implemented by:
a. forming work networks;
b. conducting coordination, integration, and synchronization of prevention and protection based on partnership patterns;
c. establishing a system for prevention of violence and protection of children and women;
d. conducting socialization of Laws and Regulations related to the protection of women victims of violence;
e. providing education for victims of violence and vulnerable groups;
f. organizing family support in the form of family parenting education, child parenting education, family mediation, and economic support;
g. increasing the capacity of protection services for women and children including institutional capacity development and increasing professionalism and competence of service providers;
h. increasing the ability of vulnerable individuals and/or groups to recognize risks and dangers from situations or actions that may cause violence, exploitation, maltreatment and neglect; and
I. increasing the utilization of communication, information, and education media.
4. Prevention and protection efforts as referred to in paragraph (3) are carried out in an integrated manner by the Regional Government coordinated by the Regional Apparatus responsible for women's empowerment and child protection.

Article 32

Education as referred to in article 31 is directed at:
a. development of attitudes and personality abilities of children, talents, mental and physical abilities to achieve optimal potential;
b. development of respect for human rights and fundamental freedoms;
c. development of respect for parents, cultural identity, language, and their own values, national values where the child resides, from where the child originates, and diverse customs of the child's own area of residence;
d. preparation of children for a responsible life; and
e. development of respect and love for the environment.

Article 38

1. Regional Government in carrying out maintenance and care is obliged to endeavor and assist children to be able to:
a. participate;
b. freely express opinions and think in accordance with their conscience and religion;
c. freely receive oral or written information in accordance with the child's age stage and development;
d. freely associate and assemble;
e. freely rest, play, recreate, create, and engage in cultural arts; and
f. obtain play facilities that meet health and safety requirements.
2. Efforts as referred to in paragraph (1) are developed and adjusted to the age, ability level of the child and their environment so as not to hinder and disturb the child's development.

Article 41

Regional Government, community and other state institutions are obliged and responsible to provide special protection to:
a. children in emergency situations;
b. children in conflict with the law;
c. children from minority and isolated groups;
d. children who are exploited economically and/or sexually;
e. children who are victims of narcotics, alcohol, psychotropic, and other addictive substance abuse;
f. children who are victims of pornography;
g. children with human immunodeficiency virus/acquired immune deficiency syndrome;
h. child victims of abduction, sale and/or trafficking;
i. child victims of physical and/or psychological violence;
j. child victims of sexual crimes;
k. child victims of terrorist networks;
l. children with special needs;
m. child victims of abuse and neglect;
n. children with deviant social behavior; and
o. children who are victims of stigmatization from labeling related to their parents' condition.

Article 42

Special protection for children is carried out through efforts of:
a. rapid handling, including treatment and/or physical, psychological and social rehabilitation as well as prevention of diseases and other health disorders;
b. psychosocial assistance during treatment until recovery;
c. provision of social assistance for children from underprivileged families; and
d. provision of protection and assistance in every judicial process.

Article 43

Children in emergency situations as referred to in Article 41 letter a consist of:
a. children who become refugees;
b. child victims of unrest;
c. child victims of natural disasters; and
d. children in armed conflict situations.

Article 46

Special protection for children in conflict with the law as referred to in Article 41 letter b is implemented through:
a. treatment of children in a humane manner taking into account needs according to their age;
b. separation from adults;
c. provision of legal assistance and other assistance effectively;
d. implementation of recreational activities;
e. freedom from torture, punishment or other treatment that is cruel, inhumane and degrading to dignity and degree;
f. avoidance of imposition of death penalty and/or life imprisonment;
g. avoidance of arrest, detention or imprisonment except as a last resort and for the shortest time;
h. provision of justice before a juvenile court that is objective, impartial and in a closed session to the public;
i. avoidance of publication of their identity;
j. provision of accompaniment by parents or guardian and person trusted by the child;
k. provision of social advocacy;
l. provision of private life;
m. provision of accessibility especially for children with disabilities;
n. provision of education;
o. provision of health services; and
p. provision of other rights in accordance with the provisions of Laws and Regulations.

Article 54

Special protection for child victims of sexual crimes as referred to in Article 41 letter j shall be carried out through efforts to:
a. educate about reproductive health, religious values, and moral values;
b. social rehabilitation;
c. psychosocial assistance during treatment until recovery; and
d. provide protection and assistance at every level of examination starting from investigation, prosecution, up to examination in court hearings.

Article 55

Special protection for child victims of terrorism networks as referred to in Article 41 letter k shall be carried out through efforts to:
a. educate about education, ideology, and nationalism values;
b. counsel about the dangers of terrorism;
c. social rehabilitation; and
d. social assistance.

Article 62

1. To prevent the occurrence of acts of violence against women, the Regional Government shall carry out empowerment and awareness to families and the community by providing information, guidance and/or counseling.
2. In addition to empowerment and awareness as referred to in paragraph (1), the Regional Government shall carry out the following efforts:
a. opening accessibility to obtain education, training, funding, income enhancement and social services;
b. opening employment opportunities for women;
c. building community participation and concern to implement prevention and protection of women from acts of violence;
d. building and providing a complete and easily accessible information system;
e. building networks and cooperation with law enforcement apparatus, government apparatus, universities and various non-governmental organizations that work and/or care about women; and
f. opening complaint posts for the protection of women from acts of violence.

Article 64

1. The provisions as referred to in Article 63 paragraph (1), may be exempted by taking into account the following requirements:
a. the age for child workers in the informal sector work is above 15 (fifteen) years;
b. obtain written consent from the parents/guardian of the child worker in the informal sector work;
c. the implementation must be set forth in a written employment agreement between the company/employer and the parents/guardian of the child worker in the informal sector work;
d. not employed at night;
e. working time of at most 3 (three) hours per day;
f. not employed in a place or environment that can interfere with the child's growth and development process, both physical, mental, moral and intellectual as well as the child's health;
g. provide opportunities to socialize with family and the surrounding environment;
h. provide opportunities to obtain education in accordance with their talents and interests;
i. employed for light types of work; and j. provide health protection for children and one day off per week.
2. Protection for child workers in the informal sector work as referred to in paragraph (1), aims to:
a. prevent all forms of exploitation, discrimination, harassment and violence against children; and
b. protect children from activities that can interfere with the child's growth and development process, both physical, mental, moral and intellectual as well as the child's health.
3. Protection efforts for child workers in the informal sector work as referred to in paragraph (2), include:
a. providing counseling to the community about children's rights;
b. providing assistance in the form of psychological, medical and legal services to child workers in the informal sector work who experience exploitation, discrimination, harassment and violence;
c. empowering families through the provision of skills training and expenditure reduction;
d. providing scholarships to child workers in the informal sector work who drop out of school to continue formal education to a higher level; and
e. providing non-formal education and skills training for child workers in the informal sector work who do not pursue formal education.

Article 66

1. Targets of handling include:
a. children outside the care of parents;
b. children in disaster emergency situations;
c. children in conflict with the law;
d. child victims of physical, mental and sexual violence;
e. children subjected to maltreatment and neglect;
f. child victims of economic and sexual exploitation;
g. children who become victims of narcotics, alcohol, psychotropic and other addictive substance abuse or matters that cause dependency on narcotics, alcohol, psychotropic and other addictive substances;
h. child victims of pornography;
i. children with deviant social behavior;
j. children who experience stigmatization due to their parents' behavior;
k. children and women who become victims of human trafficking;
l. children and women who are in situations where the nature and conditions of the workplace can endanger health, safety and morals; and
m. women victims of physical, mental and sexual violence.
2. Handling as referred to in paragraph (1) must be carried out immediately including:
a. identification process and receipt of complaints/reports;
b. rescue actions;
c. placement of children and women in temporary shelters;
d. rehabilitation including:
1\. health recovery services;
2\. psychosocial recovery services; and
3\. legal assistance support;
e. social reintegration.

Article 70

1. Health recovery services as referred to in Article 66 paragraph (2) letter d number 1 include:
a. emergency services;
b. services for providing visum et repertum or psychiatric visum in accordance with applicable provisions;
c. follow-up services in the form of outpatient and/or inpatient care in accordance with medical provisions;
d. deoxyribose nucleic acid verification services;
e. delivery process services for pregnancies resulting from sexual violence and/or rape;
f. provision of further referral services in accordance with the victim's circumstances and condition; and
g. provision of special rooms separated from general patients.
2. The Regional Apparatus Organization responsible for health affairs is obligated to provide health recovery services.

Article 71

1. Psychosocial recovery services as referred to in Article 66 paragraph (2) letter d number 2 include:
a. psychological and social assessment as the basis for preparing treatment to be provided;
b. counseling;
c. psychological therapy and social rehabilitation programs;
d. mental and spiritual guidance;
e. assistance; and
f. provision of conducive and representative places for implementing psychosocial recovery service activities.
2. The Regional Apparatus Organization responsible for social affairs is obligated to provide psychosocial recovery services.

Article 73

1. Social reintegration in the form of post-rehabilitation service support as referred to in Article 66 paragraph (2) letter e includes:
a. tracing family members;
b. reuniting children with family members/substitute families and/or the community;
c. facilitation of providing psychosocial assistance to families;
d. support for access to further education or health services; and
e. monitoring and evaluation.
2. The Regional Apparatus Organization responsible for social affairs is obligated to provide social reintegration.

Article 77

1. The Regional Government plays a role in facilitating the juvenile criminal justice process based on the principle of restorative justice.
2. Facilitation of the juvenile criminal justice process as referred to in paragraph (1) includes:
a. capacity building of human resources whose duties are related to juvenile criminal justice and the community;
b. increased coordination with law enforcement officials;
c. development of effective methods through information support, facilities and positive activities as well as taking immediate action on gaps that encourage children and/or groups of children to commit legal violations;
d. provision and delivery of legal and psychosocial assistance services in realizing the process of restorative justice and diversion for children in conflict with the law;
e. encouraging a paradigm shift in society towards children in conflict with the law;
f. developing diversion mechanisms for children in conflict with the law in accordance with Laws and Regulations;
g. developing cooperation with relevant agencies or institutions in organizing education, guidance and counseling programs for handling children who commit criminal acts, especially children under 12 (twelve) years of age to achieve optimal development; and
h. provision of supporting facilities and infrastructure for the juvenile justice process by prioritizing the best interests of the child in accordance with the provisions of Laws and Regulations.
3. Further provisions regarding facilitation of juvenile criminal justice as referred to in paragraph (1) and paragraph (2) shall be regulated by Mayor Regulation.

CHAPTER X

INSTITUTIONAL ARRANGEMENTS

Article 79

1. The Women's Empowerment and Child Protection System is the entire process of organizing women's empowerment and child protection carried out comprehensively, inclusively and integratively starting from the stage of complaint/report handling services, health services, social rehabilitation, repatriation, social reintegration and legal assistance up to repatriation and social reintegration for women and children victims of crime and violence in their social environment.
2. The Women's Empowerment and Child Protection System aims to:
a. improve the quality of life of women, children and family quality;
b. increase the institutional capacity of women's empowerment and child protection including the development of gender and child data; and c. provide special protection for children from various forms of violence and other discriminatory treatment.
3. The Women's Empowerment and Child Protection System is carried out through:
a. providing access for women and children to education, health and other strategic sectors;
b. encouraging the involvement of women and children in the development process;
c. providing knowledge, skills, character values, morals and family resilience;
d. encouraging programs that can increase women's independence in the economic, political, legal, socio-cultural and other strategic fields;
e. establishment, development and capacity strengthening of Women and Child Protection institutions including complaint service units for violence against women and children as well as legal aid services;
f. improving the quality of human resources management;
g. strengthening the institutional capacity of regional gender and child mainstreaming;
h. strengthening and developing gender and child data systems; and
i. carrying out promotive, preventive, curative and rehabilitative efforts in accordance with Laws and Regulations.
4. In order to achieve comprehensive, inclusive and integrative women's empowerment and child protection in the region, the Regional Government shall establish:
a. P2TP2A or similar institutions that have the same functions and roles;
b. regional child protection commission or other similar institutions to support supervision of the implementation of child protection in the region; and
c. other institutions according to needs and/or provisions of Laws and Regulations.

Article 83

1. The Regional Child Protection Commission has the following duties:
a. supervising the implementation of child protection and fulfillment of children's rights;
b. providing input and proposals in formulating policies on the implementation of child protection;
c. collecting data and information regarding child protection;
d. receiving and conducting reviews of public complaints regarding violations of children's rights;
e. conducting mediation on disputes regarding violations of children's rights;
f. cooperating with institutions established by the community in the field of child protection;
g. providing reports to the authorities regarding alleged violations of the Child Protection Law; and
h. other duties in accordance with the provisions of Laws and Regulations.
2. Further provisions regarding the Regional Child Protection Commission shall be regulated by Mayor Regulation.

Article 86

The development of CFC as referred to in Article 85, shall be implemented based on the following principles:
a. good governance, namely transparency, accountability, participation, information disclosure, and rule of law;
b. non-discrimination, namely not differentiating ethnicity, race, religion, gender, language, political views, national origin, economic status, physical or psychological condition of the child, or other factors;
c. best interests of the child, namely making what is best for the child as the primary consideration in every policy, program, and activity;
d. right to life, survival, and development of the child, namely guaranteeing the right to life, survival and development of the child to the maximum extent possible; and
e. respect for the views of the child, namely recognizing and ensuring that every child who has the ability to express their opinion, is given the opportunity to express their views freely on all matters that affect them.

Article 87

The development of CFC is directed towards the fulfillment of children's rights which include:
a. civil rights and freedoms;
b. family environment and alternative care;
c. basic health and welfare;
d. education, leisure time utilization, and cultural activities; and
e. special protection.

Article 88

1. In order to realize CFC, stages of CFC development are prepared which include:
a. preparation;
b. planning;
c. implementation;
d. monitoring;
e. evaluation; and
f. reporting.
2. In every stage of CFC development, the views of children obtained through child consultation must be considered.

Article 90

1. The main tasks of the CFC Task Force are as follows:
a. coordinating various efforts for CFC development;
b. preparing RAD for CFC development;
c. carrying out socialization, advocacy and communication of CFC development;
d. conducting monitoring of the implementation of policies, programs and activities in the RAD for CFC development;
e. conducting evaluation at the end of each year on the implementation of policies, programs and activities in the RAD for CFC development; and.
f. submitting reports to the Mayor.
2. Further provisions regarding the CFC Task Force shall be regulated by Mayor's Regulation.

CHAPTER XVI

PROHIBITIONS

Article 98

Every person is prohibited from:
a. receiving child visitors and employing children in discotheque businesses, nightclubs, bars, karaoke, pubs/music houses, massage parlors and steam baths/saunas, and/or other worst forms of child labor places.
b. producing, selling and/or promoting cigarettes in the school environment.
c. selling and/or promoting cigarettes, gambling equipment, contraceptives and goods that can damage the morals of children.
d. broadcasting and showing pornographic acts in community activities that can be accessed by children.
e. receiving child visitors during school hours or allowing child visitors after nine o'clock at night to access the internet at internet cafes.
f. refusing complaints and/or collecting fees in providing services to victims at Protection Service Institutions.
g. treating children in a discriminatory manner that results in children experiencing losses both material and moral thereby hindering their social function;
h. treating children with disabilities in a discriminatory manner;
i. placing, allowing, involving, ordering the involvement of children in situations of maltreatment and neglect;
j. placing, allowing, committing, ordering the commission of or participating in committing violence against children;
k. committing violence or threat of violence forcing a child to have sexual intercourse with him or with another person;
l. committing violence or threat of violence, coercing, committing fraud, committing a series of lies, or persuading a child to commit or allow obscene acts to be committed;
m. placing, allowing, committing, ordering the commission of or participating in committing kidnapping, sale, and/or trafficking of children;
n. obstructing children from enjoying their own culture, acknowledging and practicing their religious teachings and/or using their own language without neglecting access to community and cultural development;
o. recruiting or exploiting children for military and/or other interests, and leaving children without protection of life;
p. placing, allowing, committing, ordering the commission of, or participating in committing economic and/or sexual exploitation of children;
q. intentionally placing, allowing, involving, ordering the involvement of children in the abuse, as well as production and distribution of narcotics and/or psychotropic substances; and
r. intentionally placing, allowing, involving, ordering the involvement of children in the abuse, as well as production and distribution of alcohol and other addictive substances.

CHAPTER XVII

PROVISIONS ON INVESTIGATION

Article 99

1. Certain Civil Servant Officials within the Regional Government are given special authority as investigators to conduct investigations into criminal acts regulated in this Regional Regulation as referred to in the Criminal Procedure Code.
2. The investigative authority as referred to in paragraph (1), is as follows:
a. receiving, seeking, collecting, and examining information or reports concerning criminal acts of child and women protection so that such information or reports become more complete and clear;
b. examining, seeking, and collecting information regarding individuals or entities about the truth of acts committed in connection with criminal acts of child and women protection;
c. requesting information and evidence from individuals or entities in connection with criminal acts of child and women protection;
d. examining books, records, and other documents concerning criminal acts of child and women protection;
e. conducting searches to obtain evidence of bookkeeping, recording, and other documents, as well as conducting seizures of such evidence;
f. requesting assistance from experts in the framework of carrying out the task of investigating criminal acts of child and women protection;
g. ordering someone to stop and/or prohibiting someone from leaving a room or place when an examination is in progress and examining the identity of persons, objects, and/or documents carried;
h. photographing persons related to criminal acts of child and women protection;
i. summoning persons to be heard for their statements and examined as suspects or witnesses;
j. terminating the investigation; and/or
k. taking other actions necessary for the smooth investigation of criminal acts of child and women protection in accordance with the provisions of Laws and Regulations.
3. In the event that the investigation meets the elements as referred to in paragraph (1), the Investigator notifies the commencement of the investigation and submits the results of the investigation to the Public Prosecutor through the Investigator Official of the Police of the Republic of Indonesia, in accordance with the provisions stipulated in the Criminal Procedure Code.
